update public.curriculum_units as c set description = v.description from (values
('3f625fa2-6dab-4668-b6c9-defa34115bfb'::uuid, 'The general role of respiration leads to a closer look at the process that uses oxygen to release energy from glucose. This theme introduces the requirements, products and cellular location of aerobic respiration, preparing you to interpret its word and chemical equations.'),
('5d821b5a-64fd-4151-b692-7fda36b4aa23'::uuid, 'When oxygen is available, many cells release energy from glucose through aerobic respiration. This section examines the overall process, its requirements and products, and where it takes place in the cell. You will connect the chemical equation with the large energy yield that supports sustained cellular activity.'),
('696b6e8e-f218-4ed0-856d-af8a28f82723'::uuid, 'Gas exchange allows organisms to obtain oxygen and remove carbon dioxide. In this lesson, you will explain how diffusion supports gas exchange, identify the features of an efficient exchange surface and compare the needs of plants and animals. You will connect these adaptations with the requirements of cellular respiration.'),
('778ba5ef-1660-4db2-8c83-79c667c93e1c'::uuid, 'Aerobic respiration depends on oxygen, but cells also have ways to release energy when oxygen is unavailable. This theme focuses on anaerobic respiration, the different products formed in yeast and animal muscles, and why the process releases less energy than aerobic respiration.'),
('7f6ec89f-ea9a-4189-9c24-a382e3be282e'::uuid, 'The comparison of aerobic and anaerobic respiration shows why oxygen supply matters to many cells. This theme introduces gas exchange and the adaptations that allow oxygen to enter an organism while carbon dioxide leaves. It prepares you to examine how different organisms meet these needs.'),
('82b689d4-1419-4658-8921-a1f05cc132ea'::uuid, 'Respiration supplies the energy that cells use to carry out their activities. In this lesson, you will define cellular respiration, explain why it is different from breathing and describe how the breakdown of food releases energy. You will relate this process to everyday activities such as movement, growth and active transport.'),
('9ef6fcff-71ad-4d7c-8a1c-6ddaa8ab5753'::uuid, 'Anaerobic respiration releases energy from glucose without using oxygen. In this lesson, you will compare the products formed in yeast and muscle cells, write the relevant word equations and explain why anaerobic respiration releases less energy than aerobic respiration. You will also relate it to fermentation and strenuous muscular activity.'),
('a17fe4fc-d42f-4fa0-8ac0-cc57c212e3cd'::uuid, 'Living cells require energy for processes such as movement, growth, transport and the building of new substances. This section introduces respiration as the set of reactions through which cells release usable energy from food. You will distinguish respiration from breathing and see why energy release is essential to the activities of both plants and animals.'),
('bacee7a7-8a73-493f-ba3c-5194367683f6'::uuid, 'Respiration uses oxygen in its aerobic form and produces carbon dioxide, so organisms need ways to exchange these gases with their surroundings. This section examines the surfaces and structures involved in gas exchange in plants and animals. You will relate their features to efficient diffusion and the demands of respiration.'),
('c9eb2105-0788-486a-b2d0-7e94dd0aef7d'::uuid, 'Having considered why cells need energy, examine how that energy is made available. This theme introduces cellular respiration, the role of glucose and the release of energy in living cells. It establishes the ideas needed to compare respiration that uses oxygen with respiration that takes place without it.'),
('e97c17f1-a3d9-4de0-b0ea-24c72a9a710a'::uuid, 'Aerobic respiration releases energy from glucose using oxygen. In this lesson, you will state the word equation, identify carbon dioxide and water as the products, and explain the role of mitochondria in the process. You will understand why aerobic respiration can provide a substantial supply of energy for cell activities.'),
('f3463774-8bac-4ae1-a49a-9b6a306ce233'::uuid, 'Cells may still release energy when oxygen is absent or in short supply, but the process is less complete. This section examines anaerobic respiration in organisms such as yeast and in muscle cells during strenuous activity. You will compare its products and energy yield with those of aerobic respiration.')
) as v(id,description) where c.id=v.id and c.description is distinct from v.description;
